---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/discount-discipline';
const title = 'SMS discount discipline';
const desc = 'Decide whether an SMS incentive earns more contribution than the orders it discounts. Separate the effect of a reminder from the effect of its offer before expanding a recovery flow.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">SMS operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">

<h2>Specify the offer before writing the text</h2>
<p>Record eligible customers, products, subscription exclusions, expiry, redemption limit, minimum spend, shipping treatment and permitted combinations. Include the expected cost of returns and the margin of the actual basket. A percentage that is affordable on one SKU can erase contribution on a mixed cart.</p>
<p>Assign one owner to the offer across email, SMS, paid ads and the storefront. If a customer sees several codes, measure the final discount redeemed on the order rather than assuming the SMS code was the only incentive. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">coordination guide</a> to map competing flows.</p>
<h2>Check Shopify combinations in the real cart</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">Shopify's combination documentation</a> distinguishes product, order and shipping discounts and lists eligibility conditions for combinations. Do not assume two advertised percentages simply add together or that every discount class combines on every store. Review both discount settings and any app-generated savings.</p>
<p>Before activation, propose checkout cases for an eligible item, an excluded item, a mixed cart, an existing automatic promotion, shipping savings and a returning customer who already redeemed the offer. Confirm expiry and subscription treatment in the actual configuration. These are acceptance checks to run, not tests performed for this page. If a combination unexpectedly changes the payable total, resolve it before scheduling the message.</p>
<h2>A hypothetical margin calculation</h2>
<p>Suppose a basket has $90 of merchandise before tax, $36 product cost, $9 fulfillment and payment cost, and a $9 incentive. Contribution after those modeled costs is $90 − $36 − $9 − $9 = $36 per order. This deliberately simplified example excludes returns, overhead and taxes; replace the assumptions with your accounting treatment. Do not subtract the incentive again if your starting revenue is already net of discounts.</p>
<p>Now propose equal randomized groups of 1,000 eligible customers. If a no-SMS group produced 40 orders at $45 contribution each, its total would be $1,800. If an SMS-with-offer group produced 50 orders at $36 contribution each, its total would also be $1,800 before messaging. At a hypothetical total campaign messaging cost of $30, the offer group would finish at $1,770: $30 below control despite more orders. These are invented inputs explaining the decision, not merchant results or supplier prices.</p>
<p>The useful metric is contribution per eligible assigned customer over the same window. Keep people in their original group for analysis, including those who never click. Comparing redeemers with nonredeemers would select different kinds of shoppers and cannot isolate the incentive's effect.</p>
<h2>Separate reminder value from coupon value</h2>
<p>A proposed experiment can compare no SMS, a reminder without an incentive, and a reminder with the planned incentive. Use the same eligibility criteria and observation window; keep competing promotions balanced. Choose group sizes and duration based on the effect you need to detect and the available population, not a universal send threshold.</p>
<p>If volume cannot support a useful experiment, document the uncertainty and limit exposure. Attributed revenue, coupon redemptions and a before/after chart are useful operational observations, but they do not establish what would have happened without the offer. Check later full-price repeat purchases and returns before declaring a recovery discount healthy.</p>
<h2>Make exclusions enforceable at send and checkout</h2>
<p>Remove recent purchasers from the pending recovery attempt. Exclude products whose contribution cannot support the offer and prevent subscription discounts from receiving an unintended second incentive. A segment exclusion alone may not stop a code being forwarded; configure redemption restrictions where needed and explain the terms clearly.</p>
<p>After the offer expires, stop pending messages and remove stale landing-page claims. If a sale's inventory is exhausted, cancel the promotion rather than letting customers click into an unavailable promise. Track support requests about rejected codes alongside redemption figures; a low-cost message can still create expensive service work.</p>
<h2>Set a decision rule with the finance owner</h2>
<p>Before launch, agree which costs count, the measurement window, an acceptable uncertainty range and who can stop the campaign. Reconcile delivered orders, cancellations, refunds and actual discounts from the order system. Keep provider charges in the same worksheet, including segment and carrier charges where the contract applies.</p>
<p>Do not respond to weak results by automatically escalating from one discount to a larger one. Investigate stock, delivery charges, code failures and product fit first. A reminder may solve an interruption; it cannot make an unsuitable purchase worthwhile for the customer. Suppress people who revoke permission regardless of the modeled margin.</p>
<h2>Choose an app for controllable offers</h2>
<p>Ask for a demonstration of unique-code creation, expiry, eligibility, send-time exclusions, order-level discount export and holdout assignment. Check how it handles a code-generation failure and whether the message can be stopped before sending a broken link. Require evidence from your configured workflow rather than an unsupported app ranking.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy's current paid-plan offering includes an SMS/MMS beta</a>. Confirm Shopify discount and order-event behavior directly before assigning it offer ownership; email automation and advertised SMS steps do not establish checkout integration. The same requirement applies to a dedicated SMS platform.</p>
<p>The handoff is an approved offer specification, proposed checkout checks, contribution worksheet and predeclared stop/expand decision. Keep <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">message pricing</a> separate from incentive economics: a cheaper send does not rescue an offer whose incremental contribution is negative.</p>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
